  "frames": [
    {
      "id": "recall-latar-belakang",
      "section": "RECALL",
      "title": "BACKGROUND OVERVIEW",
      "body": "- Problem: a rigid TPS is capped by the fairing, so the entry mass is capped with it.\n- An inflatable decelerator packs small and inflates: large area, high drag, milder heating.\n- Trade: a larger envelope buys drag and pays heat, which is why the envelope is optimised.\n- Scope: this work treats the optimised geometry only, not the packing of the inflatable.",
      "target_position": "immediately after the outline.",
      "figure": {
        "path": "figures/thesis_HIADsDeployed.png",
        "caption": "HIADs when deployed: the inflated toroidal stack that replaces a rigid TPS on the vehicle.",
        "source": "Source: CurrentThesisFinalReport/figures/HIADsDeployed.png, thesis Chapter I."
      },
      "note": "Filled from the thesis. The narration is taken from the thesis chapters named in the figure source line, and the graphic is imported from the thesis figure archive.",
      "revision_note": "1. Bigger font or easier to read\n2. Problem is missing? (after Literature review analysis) [Rigid?] Pros and Cons (if we Geometry Increase it or Shrink it)\n\nOh and is it for optimized geometry (Is it Packable and inflatable? (Scope Elaborate, we only focus on optimized geometry on this slide))\n\n3. Scope",
      "subsection": "Recall: The Problem"
    },
    {
      "id": "recall-tujuan-scope",
      "section": "RECALL",
      "title": "OBJECTIVE AND RESEARCH SCOPE",
      "body": "- Objective: quantify how the surface and envelope of a HIAD set its aerothermal load and its survivability.\n- Method: geometry generation, DSMC analysis, validation against IRVE-3, then optimisation of the envelope.\n- Rigid against inflatable: the rigid body is fairing limited, the inflatable trades packing volume for entry area.\n- Limit: the toroid baseline as a fully inflated rigid body, Earth air, Knudsen number above 0.01.",
      "target_position": "immediately after PLACEHOLDER 1.",
      "figure": {
        "path": "figures/thesis_hiad_design_spec.png",
        "caption": "The HIAD as an overview: the inflatable aeroshell against the rigid body it replaces, as the thesis draws it.",
        "source": "Source: Hiad_DesignSpec.png from CurrentThesisFinalReport/figures, the vehicle specification drawing of Chapter III."
      },
      "note": "Filled from the thesis. The narration is taken from the thesis chapters named in the figure source line, and the graphic is imported from the thesis figure archive.",
      "revision_note": "Definition Overlap. Rigid vs Inflatable (and also Dimension Difference we are not)  [Rigid vs Inflatable figures]\n\nObjective 1st : Let's not use SPARTA DSMC and Genetic Algorithm on technical on methodology and chapter II and Chapter III\n\nObjective (Rephrase it) (Without per variable optimized) (and - performance analysis)\n\n(How's the featurework going on the optimized geometry)",
      "subsection": "Recall: The Objective"
    },
    {
      "id": "recall-literature-themes",
      "section": "RECALL",
      "title": "LITERATURE: WHAT THE REVIEW COVERS",
      "body": "- Hypersonic aerodynamics: continuum breakdown, shock layers and the rarefied regime above 50 km\n- Aerothermodynamics: Sutton-Graves and Fay-Riddell heating, and the IRVE-3 flight record\n- TPS technology: inflatable decelerators, HIAD and LOFTID, and their material generations\n- Geometry: scalloping, shock boundary layer interaction, recirculation and localised peak heating\n- Numerics: kinetic theory and the Boltzmann equation, then DSMC with VSS collisions",
      "target_position": "after the objective and scope, before the methodology block, mirroring the order of the thesis.",
      "note": "The five themes the literature chapter actually reviews, which the deck previously compressed into one picture.",
      "footer": "The review themes behind the method. The narration stays editable in PowerPoint.",
      "subsection": "Literature: The Review"
    },
    {
      "id": "recall-literature-gap",
      "section": "RECALL",
      "title": "LITERATURE: THE GAP AND THE NOVELTY",
      "body": "- Gap one: HIAD aerothermal work is mostly continuum CFD, invalid above 50 km where Kn exceeds 0.01\n- Gap two: design studies are manual parametric sweeps, with no geometry generation coupled to DSMC\n- Gap three: survivability is not optimised jointly on heat load, peak flux, ballistic coefficient and mass\n- Novelty: one pipeline coupling CadQuery geometry, SPARTA DSMC and a genetic algorithm on an analytic cost",
      "target_position": "immediately after the review themes, so the gap is shown before the method answers it.",
      "note": "The three gaps the thesis names and the single claim it makes against them.",
      "footer": "The gap the thesis claims, stated before the method. The narration stays editable in PowerPoint.",
      "subsection": "Literature: The Gap"
    },
    {
